    "note": [
        {
            "structuredValue": [],
            "parallelValue": [],
            "groupedValue": [],
            "value": "The anterior cruciate ligament (ACL) stabilizes the knee and is frequently injured in active populations.  Female athletes are two to nine times as likely to experience an ACL tear compared to their male counterparts.  Females’ higher levels of a collagen-degrading hormone, relaxin, may drive this disparity.  Relaxin’s effects may be modulated by estrogen and progestins, the constituent hormones in oral contraceptives.  Therefore, this study evaluated the impact of oral contraceptives on serum relaxin levels, ACL strength, and ACL expression of collagen and collagen-metabolizing matrix metalloproteinases (MMPs) using a rat model.  While previous animal models have evaluated ACL strength following treatment with female sex hormones, the drug regimens assessed have not modeled human contraceptive formulations, and no prior work has compared different formulations to determine what features of a contraceptive might make it more or less effective for protecting the ACL.  Hence in this study adult female rats were treated with one of six oral contraceptive hormone cocktails—each a scaled model of a currently-marketed human birth control pill—for four estrus cycles (20 days). \r\n\r\nOral contraceptive formulations containing higher progestin-to-estrogen ratios were found to improve the strength of the ACL.  High progestin-to-estrogen ratio was also associated with higher ACL expression of collagen I, as well as lower expression of MMP1 and the relaxin receptor RXFP1.  Treatment with a lower progestin-to-estrogen ratio formulation increased collagen III expression without significantly altering ACL strength or expression of collagen I, MMP1, or the relaxin receptor relative to controls.  For all treatment cases regardless of formulation, the influence of hormones on serum relaxin levels was not significant.  These findings indicate a potential application for oral contraceptives, particularly those with higher progestin-to-estrogen ratios, in protecting against ACL injury in at-risk female athletes.",
            "type": "abstract",
            "identifier": [],
            "note": [],
            "appliesTo": []
